      "line_start": 314,
      "line_end": 330,
      "observation": "Held Durrett p. 66 (pdf 74) calls log_2 n per play 'a fair price', quotes Nicolas Bernoulli (1713) on 40 ducats, and says 2^40 (about 10^12) plays are needed 'to start to break even'. Body 314-325 and annex 544-565 restate these as asymptotic per-play scale values, both numbers included, and say explicitly that they do not specify a break-even probability. The text does not record that the source reads them as a fair price or break-even point, and it does not carry the Bernoulli remark. The boundary (body 329-330) still says the chapter 'completes' Examples 2.2.15-2.2.16. All mathematical content is supplied. This is recorded so reviewers can decide whether the source's interpretive sentence must be kept with attribution.",
      "affects_verdict": false
    },
    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-infinite-mean-weak-normalization-C014",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/infinite-mean-weak-normalization.tex",
      "line_start": 331,
      "line_end": 335,
      "observation": "The boundary lists 'the alternate argument in XVII.2a' directly after 'Its full presentation' of Feller VIII.9. In the held Feller, p. 235 footnote 11 and pp. 564-565 make XVII.2a the alternate characteristic-function proof of VII.7 Theorem 1 (the generalized weak law), not of Theorem 2 or VIII.9. The sentence does not say which theorem the argument is an alternate to. 'The separately identified Poisson/Bessel example' has no locator. This affects routing only; no coverage claim depends on either item.",
      "affects_verdict": false
    },
    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-infinite-mean-weak-normalization-C004",
      "path": "handbook/annex/p15/infinite-mean-weak-normalization.tex",
      "line_start": 110,
      "line_end": 141,
      "observation": "Held Feller p. 236 takes s_n to be the lower bound of all s with n mu(s)/s <= 1, with no anchor. The handbook takes the infimum over s >= s_0 with mu(s_0) > 0 (body 93-109). Without the anchor the infimum can be 0, for example when the law has no mass on (0,1). Annex 138-139 calls the anchor part of the construction, and body 149 calls it 'the anchored construction', but neither says that this departs from Feller's own construction. This is a source-variant note; verdicts are unaffected.",
      "affects_verdict": false
    },
    {
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-infinite-mean-weak-normalization-C003",
      "path": "handbook/annex/p15/infinite-mean-weak-normalization.tex",
      "line_start": 96,
      "line_end": 97,
      "observation": "Uniqueness of the relative scale is proved by 'Exactly the same two-event argument with S_n/a_n, S_n/b_n'. This re-uses the argument written at annex 86-95 and names both ratios, whose quotient a_n/b_n is deterministic. It is counted as a written argument because every object in the reused steps is named. It is recorded because it is the only proof step in the four section files that is given by reference to another argument.",
